left(-\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186599391 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{6})" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186599418 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186599581 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186599811 :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-1=(x-1)(x+1).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+1)}=\\frac{A}{(x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(x-1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n1=A(x-1)+B(x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A(1-1)+B(1+1) \\\\ 1 & =2B \\\\ B & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A(-1-1)+B(-1+1) \\\\ 1 & =-2A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}=-\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-1}{x+1}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-1}{x+1}|{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-1=(x-1)(x+1).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+1)}=\\frac{A}{(x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(x-1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n1=A(x-1)+B(x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A(1-1)+B(1+1) \\\\ 1 & =2B \\\\ B & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A(-1-1)+B(-1+1) \\\\ 1 & =-2A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}=-\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-1}{x+1}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-1}{x+1}|{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186599397 :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{n}" "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n}"] [:db/cas 17592186599416 :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{n}" "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n}"] [:db/cas 17592186599775 :question/worked-solution "The first term is ${a}_{1}=-5$ and the second term is ${a}_{2}=25.$ Using these two terms, we compute the common ratio:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{25}{-5}=-5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general term can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}{(r)}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-5{(-5)}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-5{(-5)}^{-1}{(-5)}^{n} \\\\  & =-5(-\\frac{1}{5}){(-5)}^{n} \\\\  & ={(-5)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the infinite geometric series can be written in sigma notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5+25-125+625-⋯ & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(-5)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The first term is ${a}_{1}=-5$ and the second term is ${a}_{2}=25.$ Using these two terms, we compute the common ratio:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{25}{-5}=-5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general term can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}{(r)}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-5{(-5)}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-5{(-5)}^{-1}{(-5)}^{n} \\\\  & =-5\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right){(-5)}^{n} \\\\  & ={(-5)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the infinite geometric series can be written in sigma notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5+25-125+625-⋯ & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\